\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The typical approach to understanding the data partitions is plotting individual features using histograms, where group is mapped to colour. Here, the three data sets displayed in Figure 1 are shown in the columns, and the rows correspond to the two variables, V1, V2. Differences between groups can be seen, and interpreted on a single variable level, such as the red group has low values on both V1 and V2 in data set C. But how the data is divided cannot be seen.}}{3}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{kmeans-histogram}{{2}{3}{The typical approach to understanding the data partitions is plotting individual features using histograms, where group is mapped to colour. Here, the three data sets displayed in Figure 1 are shown in the columns, and the rows correspond to the two variables, V1, V2. Differences between groups can be seen, and interpreted on a single variable level, such as the red group has low values on both V1 and V2 in data set C. But how the data is divided cannot be seen}{figure.2}{}}
